🤔 What's Minification? (Super Simple)
Normally, code looks like this (with spaces, line breaks, and nice formatting):

<div class="box">
    <p>Hello</p>
    <p>World</p>
</div>

Minified code looks like this (all squished into one line):

<div class="box"><p>Hello</p><p>World</p></div>

🧠 Analogy: Think of it like packing a suitcase 🧳. You take out all the extra space (line breaks, spaces, indentation) to fit everything into a smaller space. The clothes (code) are the same — just packed tighter. 

Why do developers do this?

📦 Smaller file size
⚡ Faster page loading
🔒 Makes the code slightly harder for humans to read (but NOT for search!) 
⚠️ Key point: Minification does NOT hide data!  Everything is still there — you just need to look harder.

👀 Step 1: Open the Page
Open the challenge URL. You'll see a simple page. Nothing suspicious on the surface. 🤷 

🔑 Step 2: View the Source
Right-click → "View Page Source" (or press Ctrl + U) 

You'll see the code is all on one giant line (or a few lines).  It looks like a wall of text:

<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>...</title><style>...</style></head><body><div class="..."><p>...</p><div class="picoCTF{pr3tty_c0d3_XXXXXXXX}">...</div>

🤔 "This is a mess! How do I find the flag in all this?" 

You don't need to read it all! Just search!

💥 Step 3: Search for the Flag
Press Ctrl + F (Find) and type:

picoCTF

🎉 The flag highlights instantly! 

You'll find something like:

<div class="picoCTF{pr3tty_c0d3_XXXXXXXX}">

🎯 The flag is hiding inside a CSS class name!  The developer thought putting it in a class attribute would hide it. Spoiler: it doesn't. 😄

🔄 Alternative: Use Inspect Element (Easier to Read!)
Press F12 to open DevTools
Click the "Elements" tab
Press Ctrl + Shift + C (or the arrow icon) to enter inspect mode
Hover over elements on the page — they'll highlight
Or just use the "Expand All" button (the >> icon) to un-squish the code
Then press Ctrl + F and search for picoCTF 
💡 Tip: In Chrome's DevTools, you can also click the "Pretty Print" button (the { } icon) to auto-format the minified code into readable lines!  This makes it look like normal code again. ✨


